         <work xml:id="work_d1e223220">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_ecc48643">A4:007</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_56ddc289" xml:lang="it">Sonata VIsta</title>
            <title type="uniform" xml:id="title_ba261fe3" xml:lang="it">Sonata per il cembalo</title>
            <title type="uniform" xml:id="title_dd1f990b" xml:lang="fr">Sonata VI pour le clavecin</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_d252c5aa">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_3f1cd487">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <creation xml:id="creation_49e6e113">
               <date notafter="1757" xml:id="date_1ba7ee32">before 1757</date>
               <geogName xml:id="geogName_7a3ffa74">Sønderborg (?),</geogName>
            </creation>
            <history xml:id="history_044d1b37">
               <p xml:id="idm202">The present sonata appears in one of Haffner’s twelve printed volumes of sonatas for solo harpsichord by various contemporary German and Italian composers. The third volume of the <rend xml:id="idm203" fontstyle="italic">Oeuvres melées</rend> appeared in July 1757 and included works by composers such as <persName xml:id="idm204" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm205" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">P. Fischer</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm206" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">G.G. Hertel</persName>, <persName xml:id="idm207" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">B. Houpfeld</persName> and <persName xml:id="idm208" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">H.F. Johnsen</persName>. Scheibe mentions the publication of the solo sonata in a letter to <persName xml:id="idm209" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Georg Philipp Telemann</persName>. The sources suggest that this sonata for harpsichord was composed before Scheibe’s arrival in Copenhagen in 1740 as the title pages of two of the sources, which might be related to Breitkopf's master copies, do not give his title of address. The latest source was, however, produced after he started his employment at the Danish court.</p>
            </history>
